1
00:00:00,067 --> 00:00:03,136
The F-35 should have been called the F-24.

2
00:00:03,567 --> 00:00:11,263
Under the rules, the next new American fighter after the YF-23 should have taken the next number in line: twenty-four.

3
00:00:11,667 --> 00:00:16,864
Instead, the winning demonstrator, the X-35, simply kept its number.

4
00:00:16,904 --> 00:00:18,251
And that's a clue.

5
00:00:18,291 --> 00:00:24,644
Every letter and number in a military aircraft name is supposed to follow a system.

6
00:00:25,067 --> 00:00:29,695
Before nineteen sixty-two, the Navy and the Air Force each had their own.

7
00:00:29,735 --> 00:00:35,451
The same fighter was the F four H Phantom to the Navy, and the F one ten to the Air Force.

8
00:00:35,867 --> 00:00:38,562
The old Navy name packed in the maker, too.

9
00:00:38,602 --> 00:00:43,778
F for fighter, four for the fourth fighter from that company, and H for McDonnell.

10
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:48,890
Defense Secretary Robert McNamara reportedly found that maddening.

11
00:00:48,930 --> 00:00:56,629
So in nineteen sixty-two, the Pentagon replaced them with one system, and the Phantom became simply the F-4.

12
00:00:57,033 --> 00:01:05,204
The full name has six parts: status, modified mission, basic mission, vehicle type, design number, and series letter.

13
00:01:05,244 --> 00:01:10,677
Only the basic mission, the number, and the series letter are always required.

14
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:15,928
Start with the one letter every aircraft must have: the basic mission.

15
00:01:16,333 --> 00:01:21,819
A is attack, built for hitting targets on land or at sea.

16
00:01:21,859 --> 00:01:22,983
B is bomber.

17
00:01:23,023 --> 00:01:24,049
C is cargo.

18
00:01:24,089 --> 00:01:25,309
F is fighter.

19
00:01:25,733 --> 00:01:27,756
Then the rarer ones.

20
00:01:27,796 --> 00:01:30,437
E for special electronics.

21
00:01:30,477 --> 00:01:32,705
P for maritime patrol.

22
00:01:32,745 --> 00:01:35,696
S for anti-submarine warfare.

23
00:01:35,736 --> 00:01:37,139
T for trainer.

24
00:01:37,179 --> 00:01:38,583
U for utility.

25
00:01:38,623 --> 00:01:41,367
And X for special research.

26
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:44,169
Two letters on the list are strange.

27
00:01:44,209 --> 00:01:53,472
K for tanker and R for reconnaissance exist as basic missions, but no ordinary aircraft has ever been given one the way the rules intended.

28
00:01:53,900 --> 00:01:56,322
Utility was once used as a cover.

29
00:01:56,362 --> 00:02:01,171
The U-2 spy plane was labeled utility to hide what it really did.

30
00:02:01,600 --> 00:02:05,266
And the line between attack and fighter has blurred.

31
00:02:05,306 --> 00:02:10,113
Today, an F can also be a multirole jet that strikes ground targets.

32
00:02:10,533 --> 00:02:19,063
The Air Force even gave the F to the F-117 Nighthawk, a stealth jet built for ground attack, with no official air-to-air role.

33
00:02:19,467 --> 00:02:24,300
For years, people were convinced a secret F-19 stealth fighter existed.

34
00:02:24,340 --> 00:02:31,577
In July nineteen eighty-six, the Air Force sealed off a crash site in California, and the rumors exploded.

35
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:37,797
The jet that crashed was actually an F-117, still classified at the time.

36
00:02:37,837 --> 00:02:41,155
No real F-19 has ever been shown to exist.

37
00:02:41,567 --> 00:02:48,173
Meanwhile, the A-10 is the only aircraft in today's Air Force that still carries a plain A.

38
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:56,485
Next comes the modified mission: an extra letter in front, for an aircraft rebuilt, or built, for a different job.

39
00:02:56,900 --> 00:03:01,374
The best example is the C-130 Hercules, a cargo plane.

40
00:03:01,800 --> 00:03:05,733
Put an A in front, and the AC-130 is a gunship.

41
00:03:05,773 --> 00:03:08,184
H makes it a rescue aircraft.

42
00:03:08,224 --> 00:03:10,720
M makes it special operations.

43
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:13,256
E makes it electronic warfare.

44
00:03:13,296 --> 00:03:15,285
And K makes it a tanker.

45
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:16,947
Same airframe.

46
00:03:16,987 --> 00:03:19,429
One letter changes the job.

47
00:03:19,833 --> 00:03:24,158
That's why the Air Force's newest tanker is the KC-46.

48
00:03:24,198 --> 00:03:27,067
K for tanker, on top of C for cargo.

49
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:30,011
The rest of the list works the same way.

50
00:03:30,051 --> 00:03:36,197
W is weather reconnaissance, which is why the Air Force's hurricane hunters are W C one thirties.

51
00:03:36,633 --> 00:03:38,052
V is VIP transport.

52
00:03:38,092 --> 00:03:47,645
The President's Boeing seven forty-seven is the V C twenty-five, and the President's newest helicopter is the V H ninety-two.

53
00:03:48,067 --> 00:03:50,794
L means modified for cold weather.

54
00:03:50,834 --> 00:03:54,700
The L C one thirty has skis, for landing on ice.

55
00:03:55,133 --> 00:03:59,572
And M and U look alike, but they're split by the kind of mission.

56
00:03:59,612 --> 00:04:02,673
M aircraft fly combat and special operations.

57
00:04:02,713 --> 00:04:06,394
U aircraft fly support, like the U H sixty Black Hawk.

58
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:12,112
The vehicle type sits right before the dash, and most airplanes don't need one at all.

59
00:04:12,533 --> 00:04:18,390
H means helicopter, like the AH-64 Apache: an attack helicopter.

60
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:23,546
V means vertical or short takeoff, like the MV-22 Osprey.

61
00:04:23,967 --> 00:04:25,792
And Q means unmanned.

62
00:04:25,832 --> 00:04:32,188
So the MQ-9 Reaper reads as multi-mission, unmanned, design number nine.

63
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:37,267
There's even a letter for a spaceplane, and one for the ground stations that fly drones.

64
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:43,447
In front of everything sits the status prefix, for aircraft that aren't in normal service.

65
00:04:43,867 --> 00:04:45,541
X means experimental.

66
00:04:45,581 --> 00:04:47,011
Y means prototype.

67
00:04:47,051 --> 00:04:55,912
That's why the jet that flew in the competition was the YF-22, and the one that entered service was the F-22.

68
00:04:56,333 --> 00:05:00,741
J and N mean special test, temporary or permanent.

69
00:05:00,781 --> 00:05:03,676
And G means permanently grounded.

70
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:06,801
After the dash comes the design number.

71
00:05:06,841 --> 00:05:13,060
In nineteen sixty-two, the counts were restarted, which is why the numbers suddenly got small again.

72
00:05:13,467 --> 00:05:18,202
Then the series letter marks each version: A, then B, then C.

73
00:05:18,242 --> 00:05:24,230
The letters I and O are skipped, so they're never confused with one and zero.

74
00:05:24,633 --> 00:05:27,633
There was never an F-4H Phantom, either.

75
00:05:27,673 --> 00:05:33,407
That letter was skipped to avoid confusion with its old Navy name, F four H.

76
00:05:33,833 --> 00:05:38,074
And the number thirteen has been skipped in many series, for superstition.

77
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:40,695
Then there are the rule breakers.

78
00:05:41,100 --> 00:05:45,294
The SR-71 Blackbird continued the old bomber numbers.

79
00:05:45,334 --> 00:05:53,843
It was set to be called RS-71, but Air Force chief Curtis LeMay preferred SR, for strategic reconnaissance.

80
00:05:53,883 --> 00:05:55,521
And SR is what stuck.

81
00:05:55,933 --> 00:06:01,236
The B-21 skipped ahead, because it's the first bomber of the twenty-first century.

82
00:06:01,276 --> 00:06:03,451
The next number in line was three.

83
00:06:03,867 --> 00:06:13,438
The MV-75, the Army's new tiltrotor, jumped all the way to seventy-five, to honor the year the Army was founded, seventeen seventy-five.

84
00:06:13,867 --> 00:06:20,019
The T-6 Texan Two skipped four and five, as a tribute to the original T-6 trainer.

85
00:06:20,433 --> 00:06:28,109
The F-47 was chosen partly to honor the P-47 Thunderbolt, and nineteen forty-seven, the year the Air Force was founded.

86
00:06:28,533 --> 00:06:30,871
Some numbers simply copy the maker.

87
00:06:30,911 --> 00:06:37,051
A tanker sold abroad became the K C seven sixty-seven, straight from Boeing's model number.

88
00:06:37,467 --> 00:06:43,129
And the F/A-18 Hornet has a slash that the system doesn't even allow.

89
00:06:43,169 --> 00:06:46,766
By the rules, it should have been the AF-18.

90
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:50,874
Missiles get the same treatment, with three letters of their own.

91
00:06:51,300 --> 00:06:55,191
The first letter says where it's launched from.

92
00:06:55,231 --> 00:06:55,944
A is air.

93
00:06:55,984 --> 00:06:57,617
R is a surface ship.

94
00:06:57,657 --> 00:06:58,955
U is underwater.

95
00:06:58,995 --> 00:06:59,959
L is a silo.

96
00:06:59,999 --> 00:07:03,556
And B means more than one kind of launcher.

97
00:07:03,967 --> 00:07:06,307
The second letter is the job.

98
00:07:06,347 --> 00:07:09,590
I means intercepting targets in the air.

99
00:07:09,630 --> 00:07:13,201
G means attacking targets on land or at sea.

100
00:07:13,633 --> 00:07:15,764
And the third says what it is.

101
00:07:15,804 --> 00:07:17,284
M for guided missile.

102
00:07:17,324 --> 00:07:21,336
R for a rocket that can't be steered once it's launched.

103
00:07:21,767 --> 00:07:27,390
So an AIM-9 reads as air-launched, intercept, guided missile.

104
00:07:27,800 --> 00:07:31,150
The Sparrow shows how flexible this is.

105
00:07:31,190 --> 00:07:34,019
Fired from a jet, it's the AIM-7.

106
00:07:34,059 --> 00:07:38,539
Fired from a ship, it becomes the RIM-7 Sea Sparrow.

107
00:07:38,579 --> 00:07:41,147
Same number, new first letter.

108
00:07:41,567 --> 00:07:45,402
And an RGM-84D Harpoon reads the same way.

109
00:07:45,442 --> 00:07:46,694
Ship-launched.

110
00:07:46,734 --> 00:07:48,079
Surface attack.

111
00:07:48,119 --> 00:07:49,463
Guided missile.

112
00:07:49,503 --> 00:07:51,216
Design eighty-four.

113
00:07:51,256 --> 00:07:53,062
Fourth modification.

114
00:07:53,767 --> 00:07:57,941
Now, a longer aircraft name: the HH-60G.

115
00:07:58,367 --> 00:08:02,647
The first H is the modified mission: search and rescue.

116
00:08:02,687 --> 00:08:06,183
The second H is the vehicle type: helicopter.

117
00:08:06,223 --> 00:08:11,525
Sixty is the design number, the same family as the UH-60 Black Hawk.

118
00:08:11,565 --> 00:08:13,646
And G is the series letter.

119
00:08:14,067 --> 00:08:21,276
In fact, some of them started life as Black Hawks that were modified for rescue, and the mission letter followed the new job.

120
00:08:22,000 --> 00:08:28,500
So, the next time you see a name like MQ-9A, read it left to right.

121
00:08:28,540 --> 00:08:29,867
Multi-mission.

122
00:08:29,907 --> 00:08:30,745
Unmanned.

123
00:08:30,785 --> 00:08:31,916
Design nine.

124
00:08:31,956 --> 00:08:33,283
First version.

125
00:08:33,700 --> 00:08:38,982
And if the number looks wrong, like F-35, there's usually a story behind it.

126
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:42,207
Which aircraft name should I decode next?

127
00:08:42,247 --> 00:08:43,873
Tell me in the comments.
